{ "data": { "text": "The AAL atlas was utilized to segment each subject's whole-brain rs-fMRI into 116 regions (90 cortical/subcortical regions [45 for each hemisphere] and 26 cerebellar/vermis regions). Within each AAL region, an average time series was calculated.\n\nFrom the time series matrix of size 1200 · 116 (time points · AAL regions), FC matrices (116 · 116) were derived for each subject by applying Pearson correlation across the whole duration of the time series.\n\nThe number of FC was reduced from 116 · 116 = 13,456 to 6670 by removing duplicate FC present in the symmetric FC matrix. All FC were then Fisher's z -transformed, rearranged to FC row vectors, and the FC row vectors were stacked across subjects.\n\nEach 116 · 116 FC matrix was thresholded and converted to a binary adjacency matrix for graph theoretic analyses. A proportion threshold based on graph density was applied to each FC matrix, where density threshold ranged from 0.05 to 0.95 at 0.05 intervals. Network measures in this study were derived using the Brain Connectivity Toolbox (Rubinov and Sporns, 2010). Nodal clustering coefficient and nodal local efficiency were adopted to examine regional characteristics. For the global network matrices, the network clustering coefficient (C) and the characteristic path length (L) were calculated. The small-worldness metric was estimated as the ratio between the normalized clustering coefficient and the normalized characteristic path length; Crand and Lrand were derived from 30 corresponding random networks generated by rewiring each edge approximately 10 times while preserving the original degree distribution." }, "annotations": [ { "result": [ { "from_name": "Atlas definition", "to_name": "text", "type": "labels", "value": { "start": 4, "end": 14, "text": "AAL atlas", "labels": ["Atlas definition — Parameters"] } }, { "from_name": "Atlas definition", "to_name": "text", "type": "labels", "value": { "start": 0, "end": 3, "text": "1", "labels": ["Atlas 